---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const b41 = requireSection('B4.1');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    B4 is the HL-only chapter that moves from familiar linear structures into more specialized abstract
    data types. The central question is not only how each structure is arranged, but also why one
    structure is more suitable than another for a given task.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Start by separating the abstract idea from the implementation. Then compare how each structure
      stores data, how it retrieves data, and what trade-offs appear when the collection grows. That
      sequence makes later evaluation questions much easier.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b41}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B4.1.1"
    summary="Explain the properties and purpose of ADTs by focusing on the operations a structure supports, rather than on one single implementation."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      An abstract data type is defined by what it does and what operations it supports, not by the
      exact way it is stored in memory. That distinction matters because the same abstract behavior
      can often be implemented in more than one concrete way.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-properties-and-purpose-of-adts">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">ADT idea</th>
          <th scope="col">What stays abstract</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is useful</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Behavior over storage</td>
          <td>The permitted operations and expected results</td>
          <td>Lets programmers reason about use without committing to one implementation too early</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Implementation flexibility</td>
          <td>The internal representation may vary</td>
          <td>Allows the same idea to be optimized for different contexts</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Interface consistency</td>
          <td>The user of the structure relies on the operations, not internal details</td>
          <td>Improves modularity and maintainability</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-adt-vs-implementation">Worked example: ADT vs implementation</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">ADT</th>
          <th scope="col">Core operations</th>
          <th scope="col">Possible implementation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Stack</td>
          <td>Push, pop, peek</td>
          <td>Array-based or linked implementation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Set</td>
          <td>Add, remove, membership test</td>
          <td>Hash-based or tree-based implementation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Linked list</td>
          <td>Traverse, insert, delete</td>
          <td>Nodes connected by references</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The operations define the ADT. The internal structure defines one implementation choice. That
      is why the same abstract behavior can appear in different programming environments with slightly
      different underlying mechanics.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B4.1.2, B4.1.3"
    summary="Evaluate linked lists through their trade-offs, then construct and apply singly, doubly, and circular linked lists by following node and pointer relationships carefully."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Linked lists store data in nodes connected by references rather than by contiguous indexed
      positions. This makes them dynamic and flexible, but it also changes what operations are fast
      and what operations are slow.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-linked-list-types-and-trade-offs">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Structure</th>
          <th scope="col">Main feature</th>
          <th scope="col">Strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Singly linked list</td>
          <td>Each node points to the next node</td>
          <td>Simple structure and efficient insertion near known positions</td>
          <td>Traversal works in one direction only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Doubly linked list</td>
          <td>Each node points forward and backward</td>
          <td>Easier reverse traversal and deletion from the middle</td>
          <td>Uses more memory per node</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Circular linked list</td>
          <td>The tail links back to the head</td>
          <td>Useful for repeated cyclic processing</td>
          <td>Traversal needs careful stopping conditions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Evaluation rule">
      <p>
        Linked lists are not simply “better than arrays.” They are stronger when insertions and
        deletions matter more than direct indexed access. If fast random access is the main need,
        arrays or lists are often the better choice.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-constructing-a-singly-linked-list">Worked example: constructing a singly linked list</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.data = data
        self.next = None

head = Node("Aarav")
head.next = Node("Yuki")
head.next.next = Node("Sofia")</code></pre>
    <p>
      This construction creates three nodes and links them in order. Traversal would begin at
      <code>head</code> and follow each <code>next</code> reference until a node points to
      <code>None</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-linked-list-evaluation">Worked example: evaluating a use case</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Would a linked list fit?</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>A music queue with frequent insertions and removals in the middle</td>
          <td>Yes, often</td>
          <td>Pointer updates can be cheaper than shifting many later elements</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A marks table that repeatedly needs direct access to item 500</td>
          <td>No, usually not</td>
          <td>Sequential traversal is slower than direct indexed lookup</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B4.1.4"
    summary="Explain BSTs by linking their ordering rule to search behavior, traversal outcomes, and the effect of tree shape on performance."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A binary search tree stores values so that smaller values are placed to the left of a node and
      larger values are placed to the right. That rule gives BSTs their search behavior and explains
      why traversal order matters.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-bst-structure-and-properties">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">BST property</th>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>left &lt; node &lt; right</code></td>
          <td>Creates a predictable search rule</td>
          <td>Search can ignore half of the remaining tree at each decision point</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>In-order traversal</td>
          <td>Visits left, node, right</td>
          <td>Produces sorted output for a valid BST</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tree shape depends on insertion order</td>
          <td>A balanced tree behaves differently from a skewed one</td>
          <td>Performance can degrade toward linear behavior if the tree becomes uneven</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-building-and-traversing-a-bst">Worked trace: building and traversing a BST</h3>
    <p>Insert the values <code>10, 5, 15, 3, 7</code> in that order.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">      10
     /  \
    5    15
   / \
  3   7</code></pre>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Traversal</th>
          <th scope="col">Sequence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>In-order</td>
          <td><code>3, 5, 7, 10, 15</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pre-order</td>
          <td><code>10, 5, 3, 7, 15</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Post-order</td>
          <td><code>3, 7, 5, 15, 10</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        A BST is not automatically balanced. The binary-search rule still holds in an unbalanced tree,
        but search paths may become much longer.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B4.1.5"
    summary="Construct and apply sets as an ADT by using uniqueness, membership testing, and standard set operations to solve realistic problems."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A set stores unique values only. Order is not the priority. The strength of a set lies in
      checking membership quickly and combining collections through operations such as union,
      intersection, and difference.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-set-operations-and-purpose">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Operation</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Example question it answers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Union</td>
          <td>Elements in either set</td>
          <td>Who is in robotics or coding?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Intersection</td>
          <td>Elements in both sets</td>
          <td>Who is in both clubs?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Difference</td>
          <td>Elements in one set but not the other</td>
          <td>Who is in robotics but not coding?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Membership test</td>
          <td>Checks whether a value is already present</td>
          <td>Has this user already voted?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-applying-set-operations">Worked example: applying set operations</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">robotics = {"Ava", "Noah", "Liam", "Mia"}
coding = {"Mia", "Liam", "Zoe"}

both = robotics.intersection(coding)
either = robotics.union(coding)
only_robotics = robotics.difference(coding)</code></pre>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Expression</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>both</code></td>
          <td><code>{"Liam", "Mia"}</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>either</code></td>
          <td><code>{"Ava", "Noah", "Liam", "Mia", "Zoe"}</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>only_robotics</code></td>
          <td><code>{"Ava", "Noah"}</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B4.1.6"
    summary="Explain the core principles of ADTs by linking interface versus implementation to hash-based storage, collisions, load factor, and rehashing."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      The core principles of ADTs are about choosing structures for their behavior, not just naming
      them. In this chapter, hash-based storage is a useful example because it shows how an abstract
      idea such as fast retrieval depends on careful implementation choices.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-core-principles-of-adts">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Principle</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Representative example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Interface over representation</td>
          <td>The user relies on supported operations, not on hidden storage detail</td>
          <td>Using set membership without needing to know the exact internal structure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hashing</td>
          <td>A key is converted into a storage index</td>
          <td>Using a student ID to find an array position</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Collision handling</td>
          <td>Different keys may map to the same index and need a resolution strategy</td>
          <td>Chaining or probing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Load factor and rehashing</td>
          <td>Performance drops as the table fills, so resizing may be needed</td>
          <td>Creating a larger table and redistributing entries</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-hash-table-collision">Worked example: a simple collision</h3>
    <p>Suppose a table of size <code>10</code> uses <code>index = key % 10</code>.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Key</th>
          <th scope="col">Computed index</th>
          <th scope="col">Outcome</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>42</code></td>
          <td><code>2</code></td>
          <td>Stored at index 2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>32</code></td>
          <td><code>2</code></td>
          <td>Collision at index 2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>17</code></td>
          <td><code>7</code></td>
          <td>Stored at index 7</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      If chaining is used, the conflicting values are stored together at the same slot through a linked
      structure. If probing is used, the program searches for another available slot. In both cases,
      the core idea is the same: the abstract goal is fast retrieval, but the implementation must deal
      with collisions safely.
    </p>

  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
